INT. GARAGE - TOMATO CANNING DAY - MORNING

Open door, hot sun. Crates of tomatoes stacked to the rafters. Two enormous pots boil on camp stoves. Mason jars gleam in rows on a folding table.

AUNT PRUDENCE, 70, in a flowered apron, stirs a pot with an oar-sized spoon. Her sister HORTENSE, 68, in an identical apron, slices tomatoes by the hundred. Cousin LEANDRO, 30s, carries a crate. INEZ, 9, sits on an upturned bucket labelling jars with a marker. NOAH, 16, scrolls on his phone and leans on the workbench.

PRUDENCE
Noah. Gloves.

NOAH
Why?

HORTENSE
Because she said so.

PRUDENCE
She means me.

HORTENSE
She means both of us.

Inez giggles. Prudence glares at her. She hides her face in the jar.

Leandro sets the crate down with a crash. A tomato bursts. Juice sprays Hortense's apron. She looks at him. He looks at the tomato. She laughs, then she does not, and she picks up the knife again. Prudence turns from the pot. She watches Hortense, and she lowers the spoon.

PRUDENCE
You've gone quiet.

HORTENSE
I'm cutting.

PRUDENCE
You only cut that fast when you're angry.

HORTENSE
She's got a pot boiling over.

Prudence spins. The pot foams. She snatches the lid, burns her hand, and drops it. It clangs on the concrete. Inez shrieks. Noah looks up from his phone. Leandro grabs a dish towel. Hortense grabs Prudence's wrist and runs it under the spigot while Noah backs away, scrolling again.

PRUDENCE (CONT'D)
I'm fine.

HORTENSE
You're not.

PRUDENCE
She's right, Noah. Gloves.

Noah pockets the phone, takes the gloves, and pulls them on without a word.
